    "slug": "factory-rebuild",
    "title": "Factory Rebuild",
    "summary": "A fire destroys a factory, and an owner must decide how to treat idled workers.",
    "html": "<p>You own a textile factory. One night in December, an accidental fire destroys most of it. This leaves thousands of your employees suddenly out of work, just before the holidays, in a town where your factory is one of the largest employers.</p>\n<p>Suppose you are 70 years old, and you have a large sum of insurance money in hand. Three options to consider are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Take the payout and retire.</strong> The business is insured, you have no obligation to rebuild, and you have earned the rest.</li>\n<li><strong>Rebuild somewhere cheaper.</strong> Much of the industry has already moved to places where labor costs a fraction of what it costs here.</li>\n<li><strong>Rebuild here, and keep paying the idled workers in the meantime.</strong> Full wages and benefits, millions of dollars a month, with no work for them to do and no guarantee the rebuilt business will survive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>What would you do?</em> (Note there may be other options than the three listed here).</p>\n<p class=\"dilemma-source\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/world/us/aaron-feuerstein-dies-95-paid-idled-workers-after-fire-2021-11-05/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ross Kerber, &quot;Aaron Feuerstein dies at 95&quot;, Reuters (2021)</a></p>\n<p class=\"dilemma-source\">See also: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malden_Mills\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Malden Mills, Wikipedia</a> — on what happened to the company afterward.</p>"
